content.text:
Now Projects.astro + ProjectCard.astro — the signature alternating tiles.

Projects.astro:
```astro
---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import ProjectCard from './ProjectCard.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';

const projects = (await getCollection('projects')).sort(...);
const tones: Array<'dark' | 'light' | 'dark-alt'> = ['dark', 'light', 'dark-alt'];
---

<section id="work" class="work">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">{site.projects.eyebrow}</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">{site.projects.title}</h2>
    </div>
    {projects.length > 0 ? (
      <div class="work-stack">
        {projects.map((project, index) => (
          <ProjectCard project={project} tone={tones[index % tones.length]} />
        ))}
      </div>
    ) : (
      <p class="empty-hint">{site.projects.emptyHint}</p>
    )}
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .work { padding-block: var(--space-section) 0; }
  .section-head wrapper...
  .work-stack { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  /* container 内 offset：让瓦片全宽超出去吗？Apple tiles are full-bleed.
     But our tiles are inside .container... To create full-bleed tiles, we'd need
     the tiles themselves relative to viewport. Easiest: make work-stack full-viewport
     using margin-inline: calc(var(--gutter) * -1) and padding-inline var(--gutter)?
     Actually .container has max-width 1120 + padding gutters. If tiles have
     margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw) that breaks with container... Simpler approach:
     tiles aren't truly viewport-full-bleed; they're within the container width with
     the section background... 
```

Hmm, what does "full-bleed" mean here? Apple's tiles span the whole viewport. On a 1440px screen, our content max is 1120px. Full-bleed tiles would span 1440 wide with a different bg, and content inside aligned to container.

To do full-bleed within a container-structured page: give the tile a negative margin to break out: `.work-tile { margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw); padding-inline: max(var(--gutter), calc(50vw - 560px)); }` — this makes the tile span the full viewport while its inner .container stays centered. That's a nice technique. The container inside tile then centers at 1120.

Structure per tile:
```astro
<a href class="work-tile tile-{tone}">
  <div class="container work-inner">
    <div class="work-copy">
      <div class="work-tags">tags</div>
      <h3 class="work-title">{title}</h3>
      <p class="work-desc">{desc}</p>
      <span class="work-link text-link">{ctaLabel} ↗</span>
    </div>
    <div class="work-poster">
      cover img or placeholder
      <span class="poster-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</a>
```

Since ProjectCard renders the tile, and tones need classes: pass `tone` prop; class `work-tile work-tile--{tone}`.

CSS in ProjectCard:
```css
.work-tile {
  display: block;
  margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw);
  padding-block: var(--space-section);
}
.work-tile--dark { background: var(--tile-dark); color: var(--on-inverse); --accent:... etc }
```
Wait but global.css already defines .tile-dark, .tile-dark-2, .tile-light with the token overrides. Reuse them! ProjectCard: `<a class="work-tile tile-dark">`. Then the tile gets token overrides automatically. 

.work-inner: container class + grid.

Grid: desktop `grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.95fr) minmax(0, 1.05fr); gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 72px); align-items: center;`  copy left, poster right. ≤768: single column, poster before copy? Actually natural: poster first then text? Apple product tiles: headline top center, product below. For portfolio tiles, text then poster reads well. Keep copy first on mobile too? Poster after copy could push CTA far down; either ok. I'll put poster first on mobile (visual first), then copy. Use `order`? Simpler: grid-cols 1, and markup order copy→poster displays copy first. Hmm Apple mobile: headline first then product image — copy first matches Apple. Keep copy first.

Tags (category/year): on dark tile, tags use var(--border) → border-inverse, and --surface-muted (dark #f0f0f2?) — light-mode surface-muted #f0f0f2 on dark tile bg: bright chip. Hmm. Tags on dark tiles: background should be translucent white. Override within tile classes? In global .tile-dark, set `--surface-muted: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.14)`? That would make glyph backgrounds translucent on dark tiles — good for tags and placeholder glyph areas. Add to .tile-dark and .tile-dark-2: `--surface-muted: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.12);`. Also light tile (parchment): tags bg var(--surface-muted) #f0f0f2 on #f5f5f7 — subtle ok.

I'll add that override in global.css for tile classes. Also .tag on dark: color var(--text-secondary) = on-inverse-muted ✓.

Poster:
```css
.work-poster {
  position: relative;
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  background: var(--surface-muted);
}
```
On dark tile: border-inverse ✓ bg translucent white (0.12) per above override — on #1d1d1f that's #2d2d2f-ish. Good contrast.
On light tile (parchment): bg --surface-muted #f0f0f2?? For poster on parchment, better white: `--work-poster-bg`? Just set `.tile-light .work-poster { background: var(--bg); }`. And glyph color: currentColor 10%.

placeholder glyph: `color: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 12%, transparent);` font-size clamp(64px, 9vw, 110px); weight 600; place center.

cover img: object-fit cover width/height 100%.

arrow chip: 
```css
.poster-arrow {
  position: absolute; right: 16px; bottom: 16px;
  width: 44px; height: 44px;
  display: grid; place-items: center;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: rgba(210, 210, 215, 0.64);
  backdrop-filter: blur(20px) saturate(180%);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: ...;
  color: #1d1d1f;
  font-size: 18px;
  opacity: 0; transform: translateY(6px);
  transition: ...;
}
:hover img scale 1.04; hover arrow opacity 1.
@media (hover: none) { opacity 1; }
```

Copy:
```css
.work-tags { display: flex; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
.work-title { font-size: clamp(30px, 4.5vw, 40px); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.02em; line-height: 1.1; }
.work-desc { margin-top: 14px; font-size: var(--text-lg); color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.5; max-width: 40ch? }
.work-link { margin-top: 28px; font-size: var(--text-md); }
```

Breakpoints: ≤768 → single column, poster below copy with margin-top 32; padding-block 48px (tile). But AGENTS says use 640/768/1024; tile stack switch at 768 fits (Apple collapses tiles at 833→ use 768).

Wait—3 projects: tones dark, light, dark-alt. The work section header sits above tiles (on --bg white). First tile dark directly below white header. Then parchment, then dark-2. Contact dark-void then footer parchment. Rhythm: white hero → dark → parchment → dark-2 → white about?? Hold on — About section comes after work tiles. Last work tile is dark-2 (#272729), then About on white. Then Contact dark, footer parchment. Slight asymmetry: two adjacent dark tiles (work dark-2 then about white — no). It's: white, dark, parchment, dark-2, white(about), black(contact), parchment(footer). That's the pulse ✓.

But about section: should its background be white or parchment? About content on white; contact black; fine.

Empty hint: style: border dashed; keep but adjust radius/color. Fine.

Also the section-head margin-bottom when tiles present — with header on white bg, margin 64. Good.

One more consideration: `.work` padding-block var(--space-section) 0 — but the section head is inside container; tiles break out with negative margin — they'll extend to viewport edges. ✓

Also on mobile: tiles break out margin calc(50% - 50vw) stays ✓.

Now write Projects.astro and ProjectCard.astro.

